# Kashmiri (Koshur) in the Perso-Arabic script, the official Kashmiri
# alphabet. Text in Devanagari is first written in Perso-Arabic by
# src/ks.rs (`devanagari_to_arabic`) and read by this file too. espeak-ng
# 1.52 has no Kashmiri voice, so there is no reference reading to be
# compatible with: both modes give this file's reading.
#
# This file does not read the written word directly: src/ks.rs
# (`explicit`) first spells every vowel out, following the orthography as
# described in
#   the Wikipedia article "Kashmiri language" (section "Writing system":
#     the letter and vowel tables of the Perso-Arabic and Devanagari
#     orthographies, after the Kashmiri dictionary of the Jammu and Kashmir
#     Academy of Art, Culture and Languages), CC BY-SA 4.0;
#   Unicode's encoding of the Kashmiri letters and marks (U+0620 ؠ, U+0672
#     ٲ, U+0673 ٳ, U+065A-U+065F, U+06C4 ۄ, U+06C6 ۆ, U+06CE ێ; Devanagari
#     U+093A U+093B U+094F U+0956 U+0957 U+0973-U+0977);
#   Wiktionary, Kashmiri entries with a pronunciation (CC BY-SA 4.0), for
#     what the tables leave open (a word-final ہ, ٛ, ن before a consonant).
# so that each consonant letter is followed by its vowel sign (ȧ ā ə ǝ ì ī
# ɨ ȉ ù ū è ē ò ō ɔ ǫ) or by the virama (ْ), and a vowel without a
# consonant is a vowel letter (Ȧ Ā Ə Ǝ Ì Ī Ɨ Ȉ Ù Ū È Ē Ò Ō Ɔ Ǫ); ʲ is
# palatalisation (ؠ), ں the anusvara and ñ a nasal vowel. A word written
# without any vowel mark (names, loanwords: کربلا) keeps its bare
# consonants: they carry a, and the Hindi-type deletion drops it at the end
# of the word and in V C _ C V, as in Urdu.
# The phonemes are those of the Wikipedia article (after Koul and Wali
# 2006): vowels a aː ə əː i iː ɨ ɨː u uː e eː o oː ɔ ɔː, nasal vowels;
# p t ʈ ts tʃ k, pʰ tʰ ʈʰ tsʰ tʃʰ kʰ, b d ɖ dʒ ɡ, s z ʃ h, m n, ʋ l j r,
# palatalised consonants (Cʲ); x q ɣ f in loanwords as written.
# ن with ٛ (or ں): after a long vowel a nasal vowel (پانٛژٕ pãːtsɨ, سونٛتھ
# sõːtʰ); after a short vowel n before t d ts tʃ dʒ s z ʃ (اَنٛدَر andar,
# مَنٛز manz) and a nasal vowel before the others (زَنٛگ zãɡ), the commoner
# reading of Wiktionary's entries, which have both for some words; src/ks.rs
# writes ñ or ں for it. In a word without marks, ں after the inherent vowel
# is a nasal consonant of the place of the next one (anusvara = split).
# Stress: on the leftmost non-final syllable with a long vowel, else the
# leftmost non-final closed syllable, else the first (Morén 2000, "The
# puzzle of Kashmiri stress", Phonology 17: 365-396).

